ion out there and getting  some broad consensus I think we could  move move things in the right direction  another example that I've been involved  with is this one on the Great Barrier  Reef catchment and reef ecosystem  services looking at what might happen if  climate disruption is severe if the  world stays focused on on the growth  path that is on versus climate  disruption is a little bit milder if we  do get the climate situation under  control the impacts on the reef are are  both from what's happening at the global  scale affecting global sea water  temperature and an acidity but also  what's happening at the local scale  what's happening with runoff from from  the land and so that's the other axis  what happens if locally a more quality  of life focus versus an economic growth  focus stays in in play and we managed to  utilize some existing models of these  different scenarios to produce ideas  about what would happen to the coral  reef under those various scenarios under  the trashing the common scenario the  coral reef disappears under the other  other scenario some amount of coral reef  is preserved out to the year twenty one  twenty one hundred and we also managed  to Eve map those those different  scenarios again I think this would be a  very effective tool to help communicate  with the stakeholders involved what the  real possibilities are for the future  and to generate some consensus about the  preferred the preferred alternative and  then generate ways of getting there one  other example our point two is this in  New Zealand they did a visioning  exercise or a scenario planning exercise  somewhat similarly they then and the  sort of independent at Turia scenario  was the one that was obviously the the  highest quality of life they asked then  the participants after the fact where  did they think that New Zealand was in  the current period and they thought they  were generally over here they asked them  where they thought they were going and  they 